# Append-only documents: every PR adds an entry to these, so any two open PRs
# conflict on them by arithmetic. Measured on `main`: 17 of 20 commits on
# 20 Aug 2026 and 9 of 10 on 26 Aug touched `docs/decisions/history.md`.
#
# `merge=append-only` routes them through `scripts/append-only-merge.py`, which
# resolves EXACTLY ONE case — both sides inserted at the same anchor and
# neither deleted or changed anything (git's diff3 base region is empty, so
# concatenation is the only loss-free answer) — and leaves every other conflict
# for a human, with markers, exactly as today.
#
# NOT `merge=union`. Union is the obvious choice for an append-only file and it
# is wrong here: it silently keeps both sides of an edit to the SAME line, and
# silently reverts a deletion the other side reworded. `--self-test` runs both
# of those cases through git's real union driver and shows it merging clean.
#
# If the driver is not registered in `.git/config`, git falls back to the
# built-in text merge — you lose the benefit, never correctness. Register it
# with `pnpm install`, or `python3 scripts/append-only-merge.py --install`.
docs/decisions/history.md merge=append-only
docs/testing/functional-scenarios.md merge=append-only

